  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "pandas._libs.groupby.group_sum",
    "observed_behavior": "When a float sum overflows during groupby sum aggregation (e.g. summing three values of 1.797693e+308), the result is NaN instead of inf, because the Kahan compensation term becomes NaN and propagates into the result.",
    "expected_behavior": "Return infinity (or -infinity) on overflow, consistent with pure Python sum and numpy.sum, and with groupby.apply(lambda _grp: _grp.sum()) which returns inf.",
    "manifestation": "wrong_value",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "float sum overflow inside groupby sum aggregation (group_sum Cython kernel)",
      "values near np.finfo(np.float64).max such that the running sum exceeds float64 range",
      "input includes infinite values so compensation becomes NaN",
      "DataFrame.resample(...).agg with sum on float column with overflow also yields NaN"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [
      {
        "input": "s = pd.Series([1.797693e+308, 1.797693e+308, 1.797693e+308]); s.groupby([1, 1, 1]).sum()",
        "buggy_output": "1   NaN",
        "expected_output": "1    inf",
        "value_kind": "float",
        "source": "issue#60303"
      },
      {
        "input": "s.groupby([1, 1, 1]).apply(lambda _grp: _grp.sum())",
        "buggy_output": "1    inf",
        "expected_output": "1    inf",
        "value_kind": "float",
        "source": "issue#60303"
      },
      {
        "input": "df = pd.DataFrame({\"col\": [1.797693e+308, 1.797693e+308, 1.797693e+308]}, index=DatetimeIndex at 1970-01-01 with nanosecond offsets 0,1,2); df.resample('1min').agg(col=(\"col\", \"sum\"))",
        "buggy_output": "col 1970-01-01 NaN",
        "expected_output": "inf",
        "value_kind": "float",
        "source": "issue#60303"
      }
    ],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "Issue expected behavior: return infinity or -infinity in case of overflow",
      "Issue notes pure python sum and numpy.sum both return inf",
      "Fix in PR#63132 changes compensation NaN handling: sets compensation to 0 when not isfinite for float types (GH#53606; GH#60303)",
      "PR test notes np.finfo(np.float64).max equals 1.7976931348623157e+308 and it requires 3 values to trigger NaN"
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": "float64",
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": "Overflow near np.finfo(np.float64).max (about 1.7976931348623157e+308); three such values summed in one group produce NaN instead of inf",
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "Issue #60303 reports that a float sum overflow in group_sum returns NaN instead of inf, contradicting pure Python sum and numpy.sum which return inf.",
      "The issue attributes the NaN to Kahan compensation: when val is +/- infinity the compensation becomes NaN, which turns the result into NaN instead of +/- infinity.",
      "The test test_cython_group_sum_overflow (GH-60303) sums three copies of np.finfo(np.float64).max in one group and expects inf; for complex128 it sums np.finfo(np.float64).max + 3j and expects complex(inf, 9.0).",
      "The fix checks isfinite on the compensation (real part for complex types) and resets compensation to 0 when it is non-finite, for float32/float64 and complex64/complex128 sum types.",
      "Tests require 3 values to trigger NaN, per the PR body."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "The fix is tied to Kahan compensation; the maintainer noted it is unclear whether overflow can be handled consistently while staying performant.",
      "The test uses np.array of values with dtype values.dtype and group_sum called directly from pandas._libs.groupby with is_datetimelike=False, so reproduction through the public API may differ from the Cython test path.",
      "Test comment says the old numpy version needs a separate test fix, so numpy version behavior may matter."
    ]
  },
